Fallback of ghost_o_path.patch for v4.9 and v4.14.

Identical guard, identical placement, identical reasoning -- see the header of
ghost_o_path.patch, which is the design record for both. The ONLY difference is
the trailing context line: before v4.19, vfs_open() took a third argument,

	error = vfs_open(&path, file, current_cred());

so the v4.19+ hunk fails dry-run here and this one fails dry-run there. They are
mutually exclusive by construction; a builder should try _ghost's primary patch
first and fall back to this one, the same way the _hook builder lists
hide_selinux_attr_6_12.patch ahead of its fallback.

Verified at fuzz 0 against v4.9 and v4.14 fs/namei.c
(https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v4.9/fs/namei.c and .../v4.14/...).
It is NOT verified against a real 4.9/4.14 OEM tree: the builder fleet pins no
OnePlus tree older than 5.10, so there was nothing device-accurate to test it
on. Treat 4.9/4.14 coverage as vanilla-only.

--- a/fs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/namei.c
@@ -3416,11 +3416,23 @@ static int do_o_path(struct nameidata *nd, unsigned flags, struct file *file)
 static int do_o_path(struct nameidata *nd, unsigned flags, struct file *file)
 {
 	struct path path;
 	int error = path_lookupat(nd, flags, &path);
+	extern bool ghost_hidden_path(const struct path *gp);
+
 	if (!error) {
+		/*
+		 * O_PATH reaches neither ->open nor ->permission, so a path the
+		 * NoMount engine hides from this uid would otherwise be handed
+		 * straight back through /proc/self/fd/N. Fail where the lookup
+		 * would have failed had the name not existed.
+		 */
+		if (ghost_hidden_path(&path)) {
+			path_put(&path);
+			return -ENOENT;
+		}
 		audit_inode(nd->name, path.dentry, 0);
 		error = vfs_open(&path, file, current_cred());
 		path_put(&path);
 	}
 	return error;
 }
